00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:57.000
and now he hated her even more. When they were discussing getting back together, Cathy told John, if you take me back, this time it's to the death. It wasn't long before the argument started again, and they increased in violence. Most of the fights this time were over John losing his job at the mine, but John did go on to get another job. Cathy was also starting to cause trouble with John s youngest daughter.
现在他更加痛恨她了。在他们讨论复合时，凯茜对约翰说：“如果你接受我复合，这一次就是至死方休。”没过多久，争吵再次爆发，并且暴力程度升级了。这一次大多数争吵都是因为约翰失去了矿上的工作，但约翰后来确实又找到了一份工作。凯茜也开始找约翰小女儿的麻烦。

57
00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:29.000
She lived with her mother Colleen, but visited John and Cathy regularly. John s son and eldest daughter had moved out of home and had their own families. Cathy s two eldest daughters, Melissa and Natasha, had also moved out of home. But her two youngest kids still lived with her. Cathy started telling John s youngest daughter things like John wasn't really her dad, and other nasty, spiteful things like that. When John confronted her about it, Cathy was doing the dishes. So she lunged at him with a knife.
她和母亲科琳住在一起，但定期探望约翰和凯茜。约翰的儿子和大女儿已经搬出家门并有了自己的家庭。凯茜的两个大女儿梅丽莎和娜塔莎也已经搬出家门。但她最小的两个孩子仍然和她住在一起。凯茜开始对约翰的小女儿说一些诸如“约翰其实不是你亲爸”之类的话，还有其他令人讨厌、恶毒的言论。当约翰为此与她对质时，凯茜正在洗碗。于是她拿着一把刀猛地朝他扑过去。

58
00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:52.000
Cathy stabbed him in the upper left chest. John didn't seek treatment, he just put a bandaid on it, and never reported it to police. The stab wound left a permanent scar. Towards the end of 1999, Cathy enlisted the help of her nephew, and instructed him to steal John s car, which was worth about $20,000, but it was uninsured. She told her nephew to burn it.
凯茜刺中了约翰的左上胸部。约翰没有去求医，只是贴了个创可贴，也从未向警方报案。这个刺伤留下了永久的疤处。1999年底，凯茜拉拢她的外甥，指使他去偷约翰的车，那辆车价值约2万美元，但没有买保险。她叫外甥把它烧了。

59
00:25:52.000 --> 00:26:22.000
She offered to pay him $500 to do it. Not satisfied with hurting him financially, Cathy later asked her nephew to throw battery acid on his face. Her nephew refused both requests. Cathy later said to her older brother, I'm going to kill Pricey, and I'm going to get away with it. I'll get away with it because I'll make out I'm mad. Meanwhile, John Price desperately wanted to end the relationship with Cathy for a second time.
她提出付给他500美元来做这件事。不满足于在经济上伤害他，凯茜后来还让外甥把蓄电池酸液泼到他脸上。她的外甥拒绝了这两个要求。凯茜后来对她的大哥说：“我要杀了普赖西，而且我能逃脱惩罚。我能逃脱是因为我会伪装成疯了。”与此同时，约翰·普赖斯迫切地想要第二次结束与凯茜的关系。

60
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:50.000
But he couldn't work out how. There was just no way that he could see for a peaceful separation. He was also worried that Cathy might start to target his children if he ended it. During the afternoon of February 27, the year 2000, another physical fight occurred. During the fight, John thought Cathy went for a knife. Having already been stabbed by her before, he took off and sought refuge at her neighbor's house.
但他不知道该怎么做。在他看来，根本没有和平分手的可能。如果结束这段关系，他还担心凯茜可能会把目标对准他的孩子们。2000年2月27日下午，又一场肢体冲突发生了。在争斗过程中，约翰认为凯茜去拿刀了。由于之前已经被她刺伤过，他立刻逃跑，并在邻居家找到了避难所。

61
00:26:50.000 --> 00:27:17.000
John called the police. He had scratches all over him from the fight. But Cathy had bruises as well. John told the police he wanted Cathy out of his house. She had her own place in town where she could go and stay. They told him there was nothing they could do and he would need a court order. By now, the relationship had been going for six years, on and off. There had been great highs and dark lows.
约翰报了警。他身上布满了打斗留下的抓痕。但凯茜身上也有淤青。约翰告诉警察他想把凯茜赶出他的家。她在镇上有自己的住所，可以去那里住。他们告诉他他们无能为力，他需要法院的禁止令。此时，这段关系已经分分合合持续了六年。期间有过极乐，也有过黑暗的低谷。

62
00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:45.000
And that night, as had happened many times before, they made up after the argument and John went to bed. At 2.30am the next morning, Monday the 28th of February, John woke up startled. He could make out the silhouette of Cathy standing over him at the bed. Her arms were behind her back. He jumped out of bed frightened Cathy had a knife. But she didn't.
那天晚上，就像过去很多次那样，他们在争吵后和好了，约翰上床睡觉了。第二天凌晨2点30分，即2月28日星期一，约翰惊醒了。他能辨认出凯茜站在床边俯视他的剪影。她的双手背在身后。他吓得跳下床，担心凯茜拿着刀。但她没有。

63
00:27:45.000 --> 00:28:10.000
John went to work that day. He recounted the last day's events to his workmates. They had never seen him so distressed. He said he thought he was a dead man when he woke up to see Cathy standing over him with her hands behind her back that morning. His boss offered John to go and stay with him for a while. But he refused. That night John returned home and actually had a civil discussion with Cathy.
约翰那天去上班了。他向同事们讲述了前一天的经历。他们从未见他说得如此心力交瘁。他说，当那天早上醒来看到凯茜双手背在身后站在他上方时，他觉得自己死定了。他的老板提出让约翰去他那里住一段时间。但他拒绝了。那天晚上，约翰回到家，实际上与凯茜进行了一次文明的讨论。

64
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:42.000
They agreed they couldn't go on the way they were. But Cathy again refused to leave his house. That night the police arrived with the restraining order which they served on John. They took the order out as a result of the fight the night before. The 29th of February the year 2000 began like most other days. Cathy woke up early and got John organised for work. She cooked him breakfast and made his lunch. John went to work and got permission of his boss to go to the local courthouse
他们同意不能再这样继续下去了。但凯茜再次拒绝离开他的家。那天晚上，警察带着他们送达给约翰的禁止令赶到了。这是因为前一天晚上的争斗，他们才下达了这项禁令。2000年2月29日这一天像大多数日子一样开始了。凯茜早早醒来，帮约翰准备上班。她给他做早餐，准备午饭。约翰去上班，并获得了老板的批准，前往当地法院

65
00:28:42.000 --> 00:29:13.000
to apply for his own restraining order against Cathy. Meanwhile Cathy went and saw her solicitor and dropped in on a few old friends showing them the bruises she had after the fight with John. Two nights ago she told one of her friends that bastard is not going to get away with this. I'm going to bloody get him. She made an appointment with her doctor and had the injuries recorded. John saw the chamber magistrate at Musselbrook courthouse around lunchtime.
申请针对凯茜的禁止令。与此同时，凯茜去见她的律师，并顺便拜访了几位老朋友，向她们展示她与约翰打架后身上的淤青。两天前，她对其中一位朋友说：“那个混蛋休想逃脱。我非要血洗他不可。”她预约了医生，并将伤情记录在案。约翰在午餐时间左右来到了马塞尔布鲁克法院见治安法官。

66
00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:49.000
He explained the relationship issues he and Cathy had including all the violent incidents. He said he wanted to stop Cathy from being able to enter his house. After telling his story the chamber magistrate issued a restraining order against Cathy. That night John made his way to the top pub. Cathy took her children out for dinner. After dinner she asked Natasha if her two youngest children could stay the night at her house. Natasha agreed. Natasha being Cathy's second oldest child if you forgot.
他解释了他和凯茜之间的感情问题，包括所有的暴力事件。他说他想阻止凯茜进入他的房子。听完他的讲述后，治安法官向凯茜发出了禁止令。那天晚上，约翰去了顶楼酒吧。凯茜带着孩子们出去吃晚饭。晚饭后，她问娜塔莎她最小的两个孩子是否可以在她家过夜。娜塔莎同意了。如果你忘了的话，娜塔莎是凯茜的第二个大孩子。

67
00:29:49.000 --> 00:30:19.000
John made his way home about 9.30pm. Cathy arrived at his place about 11pm. She had a key to John's house and let herself in. But there was no fighting, no arguing. John didn't tell Cathy to leave. Instead they had sex. The restraining orders were just words and a piece of paper at that point. The next morning John didn't chart for work.
约翰大约在晚上9点30分回家。凯茜大约在晚上11点到达他的住处。她有约翰家的钥匙，自己开门进去了。但没有打斗，没有争吵。约翰没有叫凯茜离开。相反，他们发生了性关系。当时，禁止令不过是纸上谈兵和几张废纸而已。第二天早上，约翰没有去上班。

68
00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:46.000
Something he never did. He always turned up for work. He was reliable. He hadn't called in or let anyone know he was running late. People from work tried ringing him numerous times but they couldn't get a hold of him. They even called the police to see if there had been any car accidents that morning. But they hadn't. As he was leaving for work one of John's neighbours spotted John's car still parked out the front of his house.
这与他平时的作风大相径庭。他总是按时上班。他是个靠谱的人。他既没有打电话请假，也没有让任何人知道他迟到了。同事们多次打电话给他，但都联系不上他。他们甚至打电话给警察，看看那天早上是否发生了车祸。但并没有。正当约翰的一个邻居出门上班时，他发现约翰的车仍然停在他家门前。

69
00:30:46.000 --> 00:31:17.000
That was unusual. John always left for work before him. His car was never there at that time. John's work colleagues decided they better call the police. Police arrived at John's house at about 10 past eight that morning. Two of the first officers on the scene were Officer Matthews. I was actually working at Musclebrook that day and received a phone call at the station from a workmate of Mr Price's. And Officer Furlonga.
这很不寻常。约翰总是比他先出门上班。这个时间他的车从不在这里。约翰的同事们决定最好还是报警。警察当天早上8点10分左右抵达了约翰的住宅。现场最早到达的警员之一是马修斯警官。我那天其实在马塞尔布鲁克工作，并接到了一位普赖斯先生同事从警局打来的电话。还有富隆加警官。

70
00:31:17.000 --> 00:32:06.000
I got the call from his boss saying that he hadn't come in and somebody had been out there and that you'd been still in the driveway and they couldn't raise anybody. You know, not thinking the worst, we were just thinking, oh yeah, he's probably died one on or something. It hasn't woken up. I knew Mr Price before. I knew that he was a hard worker and a reliable man, so it was a bit unusual for him not to turn up to work and not to make a phone call. I went up, knocked on the door, didn't get an answer, saw some blood on the door handle, looked for a little gap into the lounge room which was dark, reasonably dark, and saw what looked like a bunched up curtain hanging down. I couldn't raise anybody so I decided to go and break into the place seeing as we had this complaint.
我接到他老板的电话，说他没去上班，有人去过他家，发现他的车还停在车道上，而且怎么也联系不上他。你知道的，我们当时没有往最坏处想，只觉得，哦，也许他在哪里耽搁了，或者还没醒。我以前认识普莱斯先生。我知道他是一个工作努力、靠谱的人，所以他没去上班也没打个电话，这有点反常。我走过去敲了门，没人应，看到门把手上有一些血迹，透过客厅的一个小缝隙往里看里面很黑，可以说是相当黑，看到有什么东西像一团垂下来的窗帘。我叫不到任何人，既然我们接到了这个投诉，我决定破门而入。

71
00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:49.000
Walked around the side, there was some meat lying on the ground. Anyway, went round the back, broke in through the door. As we went in I saw straight ahead what I thought was a curtain. There was something hanging, blocking my entry into the hallway of the house. I thought it looked like some type of blanket or some sort of covering that had been placed up on the archway. So I remember I used my left hand to push it aside and immediately I could feel coldness coming on my left arm. So I looked down and my left arm was just covered in blood. Initially I thought I'd injure myself breaking through the back door.
我绕到侧面，地上有一些肉。不管怎样，我绕到后面，从门破门而入。当我们进去时，我一眼就看到了我以为是窗帘的东西。有什么东西挂在那里，挡住了我进入房屋走廊的去路。我觉得它看起来像某种毯子或某种挂在拱门上的遮盖物。所以我记得我用左手把它推开，立刻我就能感到一股寒意袭上我的左臂。所以我低头一看，我的左臂全都是血。起初我以为是自己砸后门时弄伤了。

72
00:32:49.000 --> 00:33:27.000
I couldn't understand why my arm was bleeding. I realised then it was a human pelt, it was a skin, minus the head. The full skin just hanging from the top door frame. I looked past it, sort of torso on the ground without a head, without any genitalia, and my first reaction then was to turn around to Scotty and say, don't look Scotty. Of course that's the worst thing you can say. I could look through from there into the lounge room and I saw what appeared to be a human being or what was left.
我不明白为什么我的手臂在流血。我当时意识到那是一张人类的毛皮，是一张皮，没有头。整张皮就挂在顶部的门框上。我越过它看去，地上似乎有个没有头、没有生殖器的躯干，我当时的第一个反应就是转身对斯科蒂说，别看，斯科蒂。当然，那是你能说的最糟糕的话。我可以从那里看到客厅里有什么东西，或者说剩下的人形。

73
00:33:27.000 --> 00:34:02.000
And so it was at that point that I realised what had happened. For something that I'd never seen before, I'd never experienced. I had an immediate idea of what had gone on. It was at that point I drew my service pistol. There was blood everywhere, a lot of blood out from the door into the kitchen area. There's a pot on the stove. I think I might have even said to Scotty, I'll give you one guess where the head is. On the table there was a couple of plates that had meals prepared, vegetables and meat cooked sitting there.
就在那时，我意识到发生了什么。对于我从未见过、从未经历过的事情，我立刻就有了发生了什么的预感。就在那时，我拔出了配枪。到处都是血，从门口到厨房区域有很多血。炉子上有一个锅。我想我甚至可能对斯科蒂说过，我让你猜猜头在哪里。桌上有几盘已经准备好的饭菜，煮熟的蔬菜和肉放在那里。

74
00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:46.000
Sergeant Furlonga was talking to me saying it's going to be alright, we've got to keep going, we've got to finish this, we've got to do whatever it takes. He was talking to me and I was trying to hear if I could hear anybody else, because sometimes a little bit under stress, you get that auditory exclusion and you're not hearing things. You're just focused on the threat. It was quite a frightening experience, but once you've stepped into that situation, there's just no backing out. So we went to search the place and we went up the hallway. We heard what appeared to be someone snoring coming from one of the bedrooms.
弗洛隆加警长跟我说话，说会没事的，我们得继续前进，我们得完成这件事，我们得不惜一切代价。他在跟我说话，而我正试图听听能否听到其他人的声音，因为有时在压力下，你会产生那种听觉排除效应，听不到声音。你只专注于威胁。这是一次相当可怕的经历，但一旦你踏入了那种境地，就没有退路了。所以我们去搜查那个地方，走进了走廊。我们听到其中一个卧室里似乎有人打鼾的声音。

75
00:34:46.000 --> 00:35:32.000
So we knew there was someone alive in the house. Looked in there and there she was lying on the bed, tried to wake her. She was obviously drugged on something and couldn't wake her properly, she was very groggy. She wasn't responding, so we carried her outside and put her onto the back lawn. I wasn't sure if she had tried to kill herself with sleeping pills or whatever, but she certainly wasn't injured in any way. They handcuffed Kathy and tried to question her, but they couldn't get any sense out of her. There were some empty blister packs located and they worked out Kathy had tried to kill herself by overdosing on tablets. And the ambulance was called until she was taken to hospital, where she was kept in the psychiatric ward.
所以我们知道屋里还有一个活人。往里一看，她就躺在床上，试图叫醒她。她显然是嗑了什么药，怎么也叫不醒，人非常迷糊。她没有反应，于是我们把她抬到外面，放在后草坪上。我不知道她是想用安眠药自杀还是怎么的，但她确实没有受到任何伤害。他们给凯茜戴上手铐并试图讯问她，但从她嘴里问不出什么名堂。现场发现了一些空的药泡板，他们推断凯茜是通过吞服过量药片来试图自杀。随后叫了救护车，她被送往医院，并被关在精神病房里。

76
00:35:32.000 --> 00:36:24.000
Obviously, police had great fears for Kathy's youngest children. After a frantic period of searching, they managed to locate them safe and well at Natasha's house. Crime scene officer Peter Masio was then called to examine the scene. I got a phone call from VKG, which is police radio, telling us there had been a murder. I said to the operator, well how do you know it's a murder? And they said, well, he's been decapitated. We came in through the laundry at the back of the premises and there was an aroma and it was, well, it's quite a macabre thing. It was a sweet odor, a nice odor, as if mum was cooking a stew. Walk inside and one of the first things you see is Mr Price's skin or pelt hanging from the door from a meat hook.
显然，警方对凯茜最小的孩子们感到非常担心。经过一阵疯狂的搜寻，他们设法在娜塔莎的家里找到了他们，他们安然无恙。犯罪现场警官彼得·马西奥随后被召来检查现场。我接到VKG（即警方无线电）的电话，告诉我们发生了一起谋杀案。我对接线员说，你怎么知道这是一起谋杀？他们说，呃，他被斩首了。我们从房舍后方的洗衣房进去，有一股气味，那是，嗯，这是一件相当毛骨悚然的事情。那是一种甜美的气味，一种好闻的气味，就像妈妈在炖汤一样。走进去，你看到的景象之一就是普莱斯先生的皮挂在肉钩的门上。

77
00:36:24.000 --> 00:37:02.000
I searched through the pelt for Mr Price's genitals. Further into the kitchen, you could see bloodstaining on certain items and into the lounge room, there was a body. Further down the hallway, there was bloodstaining going into the bedrooms. You could see where I think Mr Price had gone for a light switch. There was bloodstaining on the light switch and also blood that he has coughed or something on the wall. You could see the expiration mark on it. But as you go out towards the lounge room, these blood stains are getting heavier and heavier. There's big swipes where his blood soaked himself and sliding against the wall.
我在皮张中搜寻普莱斯先生的生殖器。再往厨房走，你可以看到某些物品上有血迹，客厅里有一具尸体。再往下走是通往卧室的血迹。你能看到普莱斯先生去按电灯开关的地方。电灯开关上有血迹，墙上还有他咳嗽之类的血迹。你能看到上面喷溅的痕迹。但当你走向客厅时，这些血迹越来越重。有大片血迹，是他的血浸透了自己，然后身体靠着墙壁滑过。

78
00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:34.000
They're also getting lower. It comes to a combination at the foyer where he's just succumbed to his injuries. He's actually opened the front door and there's blood splatter on the screen, so he's nearly made it out. Not that it would have helped him. He would have died, certainly. You just see the enormity of where it starts in the bedroom and progressively gets worse and worse. Do you get to that point where he's died? The blood pool was the width of the hallway, which was 1.2 metres wide. When we examined the body, you could see the body has been there in its entirety.
它们也越来越低。在门厅汇集到一起，他在那里终于支撑不住伤势倒下了。他其实已经打开了前门，防盗门上有血迹溅落，所以他差一点就逃出去了。倒不是说这能救他的命。他肯定还是会死的。你只需看看它在卧室开始并逐渐恶化的巨大规模。你到了他死亡的那个地方吗？血泊有走廊那么宽，也就是1.2米宽。当我们检查尸体时，你可以看到尸体是完整地呆在那里的。

79
00:37:34.000 --> 00:38:07.000
There's a big stain on the floor where the head would have been. It's been skinned first. You can see it. She placed the skin on the carpet to sit it there and it's quite substantial staining. There's also an outline of the head, so she's taken the head off there. You can see that she's walked in carrying it into the kitchen. There's this drip, drip, drip of blood all the way leading to the cooktop. But until we actually lifted the lid off, it wasn't confirmed. To me, it was a statement. She had cut the gluteus maximus off his backside.
地上有一个大血迹，原本应该是头部的位置。她是先剥皮的。你能看出来。她把皮放在地毯上，那里的血迹相当明显。还有一个头部的轮廓，所以她把头从那里拿走了。你可以看到她提着它走进了厨房。有一滴一滴的血一直通向灶台。但直到我们真正掀开锅盖，才得到证实。对我来说，这是一个宣泄。她割下了他屁股上的臀大肌。

80
00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:49.000
It's a big muscle and she's cut that into five different steaks and she's cooked them, baked them in the oven. Two of them were on one plate, two on another plate and the fifth was out for the dog, I believe. It wasn't touched. But the meals, they were like a trophy and they had the names of each person who was supposed to get them. There were two prepared meals on the table, complete with meat and vegetables. A piece of paper was placed next to each meal. A name was scribbled on each piece of paper. One was for John's son, the other for his youngest daughter.
那是一块大肌肉，她把它切成了五块不同的牛排，然后在烤箱里烤了。其中两块在一个盘子里，两块在另一个盘子里，第五块我猜是留给狗的。它没有被动过。但那些饭菜，它们就像一个奖杯，上面写着每个应该得到它们的人的名字。桌上有两份准备好的饭菜，配有肉和蔬菜。每顿饭旁边都放着一张纸。每张纸上都潦草地写着一个名字。一份是给约翰的儿子的，另一份是给他的小女儿的。

81
00:38:49.000 --> 00:39:30.000
Officer Masio then examined the rest of the scene and collected all of the evidence. It didn't take long for news of the murder to spread. There were a few, I told you so's, and I knew she was capable of that amongst the Aberdeen community. During the course of their investigation, police found that after Cathy had murdered John, she cleaned herself up and drove into town and withdrew $1,000 from his bank account. To this day, it's unknown what she did with that money. Cathy was charged with the murder of John Price in a special bedside sitting in the psychiatric ward of the hospital she was being held in on the 7th of March, 2000.
马西奥警官随后检查了现场的其余部分并收集了所有证据。谋杀案的消息很快就传开了。阿伯丁社区里有少数人说“我早就告诉过你”以及“我知道她有这种能力”。在调查过程中，警方发现凯茜谋杀约翰后，清理了自己，然后开车进城，从他的银行账户里取出了1000美元。直到今天，她用这笔钱做了什么仍然是个谜。2000年3月7日，凯茜在她被关押的医院精神病房里通过特别床边开庭，被指控谋杀约翰·普莱斯。

82
00:39:30.000 --> 00:40:02.000
When interviewed by police, she denied having any knowledge about John's death, although she did admit she was responsible for his death. She claimed she had amnesia and couldn't remember how it happened. It became clear to police after speaking with Cathy that she was intending to mount a defence of battered wife syndrome, that she had committed the crime due to years of being the victim of severe domestic violence. Police got to work investigating that angle, and as you've already heard, they quickly realised that yes,
当警方讯问时，她否认对约翰的死有任何了解，尽管她确实承认自己对他的死负有责任。她声称自己患有失忆症，记不清是怎么发生的了。在与凯茜交谈后，警方逐渐明白，她打算提出遭受虐待妻子综合症的辩护，即她是因为多年来一直是严重家庭暴力的受害者而犯罪的。警方开始着手调查这个角度，正如你已经听到的那样，他们很快意识到，是的，

83
00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:32.000
Cathy had been in some extremely violent relationships, but she was the cause of that violence. She wasn't the victim of it. Cathy was examined by numerous psychiatrists. They determined yes, Cathy suffered from personality disorder. But a personality disorder is not actually considered a psych illness. They found Cathy's violence and rages were just part of her nature, part of her makeup. She got a sick sense of enjoyment out of what she did.
凯茜曾有过一些极其暴力的恋爱关系，但她才是暴力的制造者。她不是受害者。许多精神病学家对凯茜进行了检查。他们确定，是的，凯茜患有人格障碍。但人格障碍实际上不被认为是一种精神疾病。他们发现凯茜的暴力和暴怒只是她本性的一部分，是她性格的一部分。她从自己的所作所为中获得了变态的快感。

84
00:40:32.000 --> 00:41:08.000
Cathy's claims that she couldn't remember anything about the crime were dismissed. She was basically putting on a show. It was found that her actions were premeditated and the timing was planned. She knew exactly what she was doing. She wasn't suffering from a psychiatric illness at all. She was just pure evil. Cathy initially pled not guilty and her trial started on the 15th of October 2001. The judge assigned to the case was the Honourable Barry O'Keefe.
凯茜关于她不记得任何犯罪细节的说法被驳回了。她基本上是在演戏。研究发现，她的行为是预谋的，时间也是计划好的。她完全知道自己在做什么。她根本没有患精神疾病。她简直纯粹是恶魔。凯茜最初对指控不认罪，她的审判于2001年10月15日开始。指派给该案的法官是巴里·奥基夫大法官。

85
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:46.000
At no time as either a barrister or as a judge, did I ever strike anything of the horrific nature of Catherine Mary Knight's case. She pleaded not guilty and the trial began in the conventional way. She sat in the dock, she was quite small, unimpressive. In a crowd you'd certainly never notice her. Before the trial got going, Cathy surprised everyone and changed the plea to guilty. I was somewhat nervous about this because I was concerned that if I took a guilty plea, sentenced her, she would then appeal to the Court of Criminal Appeal
无论作为大律师还是法官，我从未遇到过任何像凯瑟琳·玛丽·奈特案这样骇人听闻的事情。她不认罪，审判以常规方式开始。她坐在被告席上，身材矮小，毫不起眼。在人群中你绝对不会注意到她。在审判开始前，凯茜让所有人大吃一惊，将罪名改为了认罪。我对此有点紧张，因为我担心如果我接受认罪并判处她刑罚，她随后会向刑事上诉法院提起上诉

86
00:41:46.000 --> 00:42:22.000
and say she was insane at the time that she made the plea of guilty. I decided that I should get a court expert to examine her and the psychiatrist came to exactly the same conclusion as the other psychiatrist that had seen it. Namely that she was not insane, she was quite sane, but she was a bad woman. Throughout the whole of the evidence, it didn't matter what the witness said, she didn't look at the witness. It didn't matter what the photograph or video was, she didn't look at that.
并辩称她在认罪时精神失常。我决定应该让法院专家对她进行检查，精神病学家的结论与其他见过她的精神病学家的结论完全一致。即她没有精神失常，她完全神志清醒，但她是个坏女人。在所有的证据审理过程中，无论证人说什麽，她都不看证人。无论照片或视频是什麽，她都不看。

87
00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:56.000
She sat looking straight ahead, absolutely impassive. There was never a sign on her face of any reaction to any of the evidence. At one point during the sentencing hearing, when some of the graphic details were being presented to the court, Cathy broke down. She started rocking violently back and forth and started screaming. She came down to the floor and started threshing about, but she wasn't fooling anybody.
她直视前方坐着，绝对无动于衷。对于任何证据，她的脸上从未出现过任何反应的迹象。在量刑听证会的一次审理中，当一些令人毛骨悚然的细节被呈现在法庭上时，凯茜崩溃了。她开始剧烈地前后摇晃并尖叫起来。她倒在地上开始痛苦地挣扎，但她并没有愚弄任何人。

88
00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:27.000
When she wanted an adjournment, she manipulated her behaviour so that she got it. Thereafter, she resumed the same man that she had before, but I didn't think that she'd switched off. In sentencing, the judge found that Cathy's suicide attempt wasn't a genuine attempt. He didn't believe that she couldn't remember anything about the crime. He determined she knew exactly what she was doing. She acted with planning and premeditation. She had openly said many times she was going to kill John.
当她想要休庭时，她操纵自己的行为以达到目的。此后，她恢复了先前的神态，但我并不认为她已经收敛了。在量刑时，法官发现凯茜的自杀未遂并不是一次真诚的尝试。他不相信她对犯罪过程毫无记忆。他认定她完全知道自己在做什么。她是带着计划和预谋行事的。她曾公开多次说过她要杀了约翰。

89
00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:57.000
She had even said she would get away with it because people would think she was mad. The judge found her to be vengeful, violent, and believed she was an extreme danger to society. He dismissed her claims of battered wife syndrome, finding that the evidence clearly showed she was the violent one in her relationships. As a result, Cathy Knight became the first woman in Australia to be sentenced to imprisonment for the term of her natural life. No possibility of parole.
她甚至说过她会逃脱惩罚，因为人们会以为她疯了。法官认为她心狠手辣、暴力成性，并认为她对社会极其危险。他驳回了她关于遭受虐待妻子综合症的说法，认为证据清楚地表明她是关系中的暴力制造者。因此，凯茜·奈特成为澳大利亚第一位被判处终身监禁的女性。不得假释。

90
00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:28.000
To sentence any person to life imprisonment is a big thing. To sentence a woman to life imprisonment for me was an even bigger thing. This was as bad a case as you got, so she had to go to jail for the term of her life. She'd shown no remorse. She hadn't acknowledged that she had any problem at all. Such a person, if released, is not unlikely to do the same again. There's a real prospect of it.
判处任何人终身监禁都是一件大事。对我来说，判处一名女性终身监禁是一件更大的一件事。这是一起极其恶劣的案件，所以她必须在监狱里度过她的余生。她没有表现出丝毫悔意。她没有承认自己有任何问题。这样的人如果被释放，并非不可能再次作案。确实存在这种可能性。

91
00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:59.000
The detective in charge of the case was Detective Bob Wells. When I was interviewing Catherine, you can catch someone's eye and you say to yourself, I'm being bullshitted here. It's a combination of catching their eye, catching the expression on their face, that you know that this person is feeding you only what she wants to feed you. And that's why I still believe that Catherine would now be able to provide information as to what took place and why it took place.
负责该案的侦探是鲍勃·韦尔斯侦探。当我采访凯瑟琳时，你可以抓住一个人的眼神，然后对自己说，我在这里被忽悠了。这是抓住他们的眼神、抓住他们脸上的表情的结合，让你知道这个人只在向你提供她想提供的信息。这就是为什么我仍然相信凯瑟琳现在能够提供关于发生了什么以及为什么发生的信息。

92
00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:28.000
After a while of doing jail time, Cathy said that she'd finally found peace and she was enjoying her time. But she obviously didn't like the idea of spending the rest of her life there because she appealed to the New South Wales Court of Criminal Appeal. She claimed her sentence was too harsh. Thankfully, the court rejected her appeal and she will spend the rest of her life behind bars. To this day, she has never given a reason for what she did or why she did it.
在服刑一段时间后，凯茜说她终于找到了平静，并且很享受她的时光。但她显然不喜欢在余生都呆在那里的想法，因为她向新南威尔士州刑事上诉法院提出了上诉。她声称她的刑罚太重了。谢天谢地，法院驳回了她的上诉，她将在狱中度过余生。直到今天，她从未对她所做的事情或原因给出一个解释。

93
00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:33.000
And she has never shown any remorse. There aren't comments yet for this episode. Click on any sentence in the transcript to leave a comment.
而且她从未表现出任何悔意。本集目前还没有评论。点击文稿中的任何句子即可发表评论。
